<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Wusun</h1><p class="st-meta">161 BCE – 500 CE (approx.) · capital Chigu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Nomads of the Ili valley and Issyk-Kul who became the Han dynasty's chief steppe ally against the Xiongnu, sealed by marriages to Chinese princesses.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Wusun first lived near the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> region and were subject to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>. Their ruler, the kunmo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liejiaomi">Liejiaomi</a>, raised by the Xiongnu, led them west around 161 BCE to the Ili valley and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/issyk-kul">Issyk-Kul</a> basin, driving out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saka">Saka</a> who had settled there.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Han envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a> visited around 115 BCE seeking an alliance. The Wusun were pastoralists with large horse herds and, by Han count, some 630,000 people, ruled by the kunmo from the Red Valley city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chigu">Chigu</a>. Han princesses, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-xijun">Liu Xijun</a>, who wrote a famous lament on her exile, married Wusun rulers, and in 71 BCE a joint Han-Wusun campaign struck the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dual-kingship">dual kingship</a> of greater and lesser kunmo developed after 53 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Wusun weakened through internal division and pressure from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate</a>, moving into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamirs</a> in the 5th century, after which they disappear as a distinct power. Their remnants merged into later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkic-peoples">Turkic peoples</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semirechye">Zhetysu</a> region.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/wusun.jpg" alt="Wusun" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Derzsi Elekes Andor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:30._Rider_burial_mound_Tenlik_(III.-II._B.C.)_Kazakhstan.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>161 BCE – 500 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chigu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Liejiaomi, Wengguimi</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>unknown (possibly Iranian or Tocharian)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, China</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wusun kingdom, Wu-sun</dd></dl></aside></div>

